    monitoring: "Pain score (NRS 0-10) at rest and with movement q4h. Sedation score if on opioids. Bowel function chart. Respiratory rate if on morphine. Watch for signs of compartment syndrome (pain out of proportion, paraesthesia, pallor, pulselessness, poikilothermia).",
    counselling: "Pain control is important for healing and recovery — do not try to \"tough it out.\" Take pain medications on a schedule (not PRN) for the first few days. As pain improves, we will reduce and stop the stronger medications. Physiotherapy and movement are also important for pain relief. Report any new severe pain, numbness, or tingling.",
    followUp: "Orthopaedic clinic in 2 weeks for wound check and X-ray. Continue physiotherapy. Gradually reduce analgesia over 1-2 weeks as pain improves.",
    pearls: "The WHO Analgesic Ladder is a stepwise approach: Step 1 (non-opioid ± adjuvant) → Step 2 (weak opioid + non-opioid ± adjuvant) → Step 3 (strong opioid + non-opioid ± adjuvant). Paracetamol and NSAIDs have a ceiling effect — increasing the dose beyond maximum does not increase analgesia. Scheduled dosing is superior to PRN for acute pain. Multimodal analgesia (using drugs with different mechanisms) improves pain control and reduces opioid requirements.",
