    goals: "Rapidly relieve pain and inflammation, treat acute flare, lower uric acid long-term to prevent recurrence",
    pharm: "1. Acute flare treatment (start immediately): First-line: Oral NSAID — Indomethacin 50mg TID for 3-5 days (or Naproxen 500mg BID, or Diclofenac 50mg TID). Add PPI if GI risk. Or Oral Colchicine 1.2mg (2 tabs) stat, then 0.6mg (1 tab) 1 hour later, then 0.6mg BID for 3-5 days (dose-reduce if CKD). Or Oral Prednisolone 30-40mg daily for 5-7 days (if NSAID/colchicine contraindicated). 2. Intra-articular steroid injection (if single joint, septic arthritis ruled out). 3. Ice packs to joint 20 mins on/off. 4. DO NOT start urate-lowering therapy (ULT) during acute flare — wait until flare fully resolved (2-4 weeks).",
    nonPharm: "Rest and elevate left foot. Ice packs. Avoid alcohol and purine-rich foods (red meat, organ meats, shellfish). Hydrate well (2-3L water/day). Use a bed cradle to keep sheets off the toe.",
    carePlan: "Start Indomethacin 50mg TID for 5 days. Ice, rest, elevation. Review in 1 week. If flare resolved: discuss lifestyle modifications. Check uric acid in 4 weeks. If uric acid >9.0 or recurrent flares (>2/year), start ULT (Allopurinol 100mg daily, titrate to 300mg daily, target uric acid <6.0 mg/dL). Start ULT 2-4 weeks after flare completely resolves.",
    dtps: "1. Acute gouty inflammation requiring rapid anti-inflammatory therapy. 2. Hyperuricaemia requiring lifestyle modification and possible ULT.",
    monitoring: "Pain score, joint inflammation (warmth, swelling, erythema) daily during flare. Uric acid level at 4 weeks post-flare. Renal function. Watch for NSAID side effects (GI, renal).",
    counselling: "Gout is caused by uric acid crystals forming in the joint — like tiny needles causing intense inflammation. The acute flare usually resolves in 3-7 days with treatment. Avoid triggers: red meat, offal, beer, and sugary drinks. Drink plenty of water. If you have further attacks, we may start long-term medication to lower uric acid.",
    followUp: "Review in 1 week for flare resolution. In 4 weeks: check uric acid. If ULT started: monthly uric acid checks until target reached, then 6-monthly. Lifestyle: weight loss, reduced alcohol and purine intake.",
    pearls: "Gout is the most common inflammatory arthritis in men. The classic presentation: acute monoarthritis of the 1st MTP (podagra) with excruciating pain. Colchicine is most effective if given within 12 hours of onset. ULT should NEVER be started during an acute flare (can prolong the flare). If starting ULT in a patient already on it during a flare, continue ULT. Allopurinol hypersensitivity syndrome (DRESS) is rare but serious — start at 100mg and titrate slowly, especially in CKD.",
    references: ["EULAR Guidelines: Gout Management", "ACR Guidelines: Gout", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Rheumatic Diseases"],
