    vitals: "HR: 111 bpm, BP: 79/57 mmHg, RR: 38 breaths/min, Temp: 39.8°C, SpO2: 85%",
    labs: "FBC, ESR, CRP: all normal. Rheumatoid factor: negative. Anti-CCP: negative. ANA: negative. TSH: normal. Vitamin D: 20 ng/mL (insufficient, normal >30). CPK: normal.",
    imaging: "None needed — fibromyalgia is a clinical diagnosis.",
    diagnosis: "Fibromyalgia Syndrome (2016 ACR Criteria met: WPI >7, SSS >5, symptoms present >3 months, no other explanatory disorder)",
    ddx: ["Fibromyalgia", "Rheumatoid Arthritis (seronegative)", "Systemic Lupus", "Myofascial Pain Syndrome", "Polymyalgia Rheumatica", "Hypothyroidism", "Chronic Fatigue Syndrome"],
    goals: "Reduce pain (target <4/10), improve sleep quality, increase function and activity level, reduce fatigue, improve mood and quality of life",
    pharm: "1. Pregabalin (first-line): Start 75mg BID, titrate to 150mg BID over 4 weeks based on response/tolerance. Reduce dose if CKD. 2. Duloxetine (first-line, especially if anxiety/depression present): Start 30mg daily for 1 week, then increase to 60mg daily. SNRI improves pain, mood, and function. 3. Amitriptyline (for sleep): Low dose 10-25mg at bedtime. Titrate slowly. 4. Alternative: Milnacipran 50mg BID (SNRI, third first-line agent). 5. Avoid: opioids (ineffective long-term, risk of dependence), NSAIDs (ineffective for fibromyalgia pain), corticosteroids. 6. Adjunctive: Paracetamol PRN (limited efficacy). Vitamin D supplementation if deficient. 7. For neuropathic pain not controlled by first-line: consider Gabapentin 300mg TID (off-label, less effective than pregabalin).",
    nonPharm: "Exercise therapy: graded aerobic exercise (walking, swimming) start 5-10 min/day, increase gradually to 30 min/day. CBT for pain management and coping skills. Sleep hygiene (regular sleep-wake schedule, no screens before bed). Stress reduction techniques (mindfulness, relaxation). Pacing activity (avoid boom-bust cycles). Patient education: fibromyalgia is real, not \"in your head.\"",
    carePlan: "Start Pregabalin 75mg BID + Amitriptyline 10mg at bedtime. Educate about fibromyalgia as a central sensitisation syndrome. Refer to physiotherapy for graded exercise programme. Sleep hygiene counselling. Review in 4 weeks. If good response, continue. If not, add/increase Duloxetine. If no response to 2 first-line agents, consider referral to pain clinic.",
    dtps: "1. Chronic widespread pain due to central sensitisation (fibromyalgia). 2. Significant impact on sleep, function, and quality of life. 3. Failed simple analgesics — requires centrally acting medications.",
