    allergies: "NKDA",
    pe: "Posture: antalgic, leaning to the left (away from pain). Lumbar spine: reduced flexion (can reach mid-shin). Straight leg raise (SLR) test: positive on the right at 45° (reproduces leg pain — confirms nerve root tension). Weakness: right extensor hallucis longus (EHL) 4/5 (L5 myotome). Sensory: reduced light touch over right L5 dermatome (lateral shin, dorsum of foot). Reflexes: knee and ankle reflexes 2+ bilaterally. No saddle anaesthesia. No lower limb oedema.",
    vitals: "HR: 113 bpm, BP: 101/99 mmHg, RR: 22 breaths/min, Temp: 38.2°C, SpO2: 93%",
    labs: "No acute bloods needed. If considering differentials: ESR, CRP, FBC, calcium, alkaline phosphatase. If neurological deficit progressing: urgent MRI.",
    imaging: "MRI Lumbar Spine: Large right-sided L4-L5 disc herniation (posterolateral, extruded fragment) compressing the L5 nerve root. Mild canal stenosis. No other significant disc pathology. No evidence of tumour, infection, or fracture.",
    diagnosis: "Chronic Low Back Pain with Right L5 Radiculopathy (Neuropathic Pain Component) due to L4-L5 Disc Herniation",
    ddx: ["Lumbar Disc Herniation with Radiculopathy", "Facet Joint Pain (axial low back pain, negative SLR)", "Spinal Stenosis (neurogenic claudication — pain with walking, relieved by flexion)", "Piriformis Syndrome", "Peripheral Neuropathy (bilateral, symmetric — diabetes)", "Spinal Tumour/Infection (red flags: night pain, weight loss, fever)"],
    goals: "Relieve neuropathic pain (target <4/10), improve functional capacity (walking, sitting), reduce opioid reliance, prevent chronicity, address psychological impact",
    pharm: "1. Neuropathic pain agent (first-line): Gabapentin 300mg at night, titrate by 300mg every 3-5 days as tolerated — target 300mg TID (900mg/day). Maximum 3600mg/day. Slow titration reduces dizziness/sedation. OR Pregabalin 75mg BID, titrate to 150mg BID over 2-4 weeks. 2. Tricyclic antidepressant (second-line / adjunct): Amitriptyline 10-25mg at bedtime, titrate slowly (max 75mg). Effective for neuropathic pain and sleep. Avoid if cardiac history. 3. Weak opioid (short-term only): Tramadol 50mg q6h PRN (max 400mg/day) — for acute flares. Strict time-limited (2-4 weeks). 4. Neuropathic pain cream: Capsaicin 0.025-0.075% cream applied to affected area TID (depletes substance P). 5. Continue paracetamol 1g QID for the mechanical/axial component. 6. If neuropathic pain refractory to 2 first-line agents: refer for epidural steroid injection (transforaminal L4-L5 on the right — targeted delivery of corticosteroid to the inflamed nerve root). 7. Avoid opioids long-term (ineffective for neuropathic pain, risk of dependence, opioid-induced hyperalgesia).",
    nonPharm: "Physiotherapy: core strengthening programme (McKenzie method, Pilates-based exercises), neural mobilisation techniques. Ergonomic modifications: lumbar support in chair, sit-stand desk if available, avoid prolonged sitting. Walking programme: 20-30 minutes daily (swimming or hydrotherapy if available). Weight loss if overweight (reduces disc loading). TENS machine (transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation) for pain relief. Sleep hygiene — adequate sleep reduces pain perception.",
    carePlan: "Start Gabapentin 300mg at night, titrate to 300mg TID over 2 weeks. Add Amitriptyline 10mg at bedtime. Continue paracetamol. Refer for physiotherapy (neural mobilisation + core strengthening). Ergonomic advice. Review in 4 weeks: assess neuropathic pain (DN4 questionnaire). If adequate response: continue. If inadequate: increase gabapentin to 600mg TID or switch to pregabalin. If still refractory after 6-8 weeks: refer for epidural steroid injection. If progressive neurological deficit develops (worsening weakness, bowel/bladder symptoms): urgent surgical referral.",
    dtps: "1. Chronic neuropathic pain requiring targeted pharmacotherapy (gabapentinoids, not just simple analgesics). 2. L5 radiculopathy with motor deficit (EHL 4/5) — monitor for progression. 3. Failed first-line conservative measures — escalation needed.",
    monitoring: "Pain score (NRS) and DN4 neuropathic pain score every visit. Functional assessment: walking distance, sitting tolerance, sleep quality. Monitor gabapentin side effects: drowsiness, dizziness, weight gain, peripheral oedema. Monitor amitriptyline: dry mouth, constipation, sedation, QT prolongation (ECG if cardiac risk). Watch for progressive motor weakness (surgical emergency if developing). Monitor for cauda equina syndrome: any new bowel/bladder dysfunction, saddle anaesthesia — immediate surgical referral.",
    counselling: "Neuropathic pain is different from regular pain — it is caused by damage to the nerve itself, producing burning, shooting, and tingling sensations. Standard painkillers (paracetamol, ibuprofen) work poorly for neuropathic pain. Gabapentin works by calming overactive nerve signals. Start low and increase slowly to reduce side effects. Amitriptyline at low doses helps both pain and sleep. Do not drive until you know how gabapentin affects you (drowsiness). Avoid alcohol with these medications. Physiotherapy with neural mobilisation is important — it helps the nerve glide more freely and reduces irritation.",
    followUp: "Pain clinic / neurology follow-up in 4 weeks for gabapentin titration and pain assessment. Physiotherapy: weekly for 8 weeks. If no improvement in 6-8 weeks on maximal neuropathic pain medications: refer for epidural steroid injection or neurosurgical opinion. MRI repeat only if new symptoms develop or surgical planning.",
    pearls: "Neuropathic pain affects 6-8% of the general population and is one of the most difficult pain syndromes to treat. The NNT (number needed to treat) for neuropathic pain medications is typically 3-5 (much higher than simple analgesics), meaning many patients need trials of multiple agents. Gabapentin and pregabalin are alpha-2-delta calcium channel ligands — they reduce excitatory neurotransmitter release at the dorsal horn. Amitriptyline (TCA) inhibits reuptake of noradrenaline and serotonin in descending pain pathways. In Kenya, gabapentin is available as 100mg and 300mg capsules (relatively affordable). Pregabalin is more expensive but has more predictable pharmacokinetics. The McKenzie method of physiotherapy is evidence-based for disc herniation and is widely applicable. Epidural steroid injections provide targeted anti-inflammatory effect to the nerve root and can provide 4-12 weeks of relief — useful as a bridge while waiting for natural disc resorption.",
